module

public import Riesz2Audit.Convergence.Definitions

/-!
# Theorem 3 (Uniqueness of the limiting measure): the statement

Mathlib-only statement of Theorem 3 of J. Boursier, *Decay of correlations and thermodynamic
limit for the circular Riesz gas* (arXiv:2209.00396, revised version 2026).

For every pair of parameters `β > 0`, `s > 0`, `s ≠ 1` (`Parameters`), there is a unique
point process `P = Riesz_{s,β}` (`UniqueRieszLimit`, `limitingRieszProcess`) to which the
microscopic laws `Q_{N,β}` (`microscopicLaw`) converge in local total variation. This process
is translation invariant (`IsStationary`) and has intensity one (`HasIntensity`).

Its Palm law satisfies `PalmRegular`: the set of configurations containing `0` and unbounded
in both directions is measurable and has Palm mass one. The Palm measure pulled back to this
set is a probability measure. The Palm measure on all configurations is a probability measure
and assigns mass zero to the complement. The law of the two-sided gap sequence is invariant
under shifting its integer indices. Finally, for every `i ∈ ℤ`, the gap `z_{i+1}-z_i` is
integrable and has expectation one, where `(z_i)` is the increasing enumeration with `z_0 = 0`
(`rootedPoint`, `rootedGap`).

## How the objects are specified

`LineConfiguration` is a locally finite subset of `ℝ`, with simplicity built in. Its measurable
space is generated by the counts in bounded Borel sets (`configurationMeasurableSpace`).
`PointProcess` is a Mathlib probability measure on this space. `HasIntensity P 1` says that the
expected count in every bounded Borel set is its Lebesgue measure. Translation by `t` is encoded
as `C ↦ C-t` (`translateConfiguration`), and stationarity requires invariance for every `t ∈ ℝ`.

`microscopicConfiguration 0` retains particles whose centered circle representatives lie in
`(-1/4,1/4)` and rescales their positions by `N`. `microscopicLaw` is its pushforward under the
finite Gibbs law. For `s < 1` that law is the imported companion law, and for `s > 1` it uses
the convergent lattice-sum kernel. `LocalConverges` includes that every microscopic law is a
probability measure and requires total variation convergence on every `[-R,R]`, `R > 0`, along
`N = Riesz1.particleNumber n = n+1`. `totalVariation` is `sup_A |μ(A)-ν(A)|` over measurable
sets. `UniqueRieszLimit` asserts existence and uniqueness of a local limit among point
processes. Uniqueness concerns the point-process limit of the microscopic laws in local total
variation.

The Palm measure is the average of `Σ_{x∈C∩[0,1)} δ_{C-x}` under `P` (`palmKernel`,
`palmMeasure`). `rootedPalmMeasure` is its pullback to configurations containing `0` and
unbounded in both directions (`rootedConfigurations`, `RootedConfiguration`). `rootedPoint` uses
infima and suprema to enumerate this set. `PalmRegular` concludes its carrier's measurability,
the support and normalization assertions, the gap-sequence shift identity and all the mean-one
integrable gaps. Existence of the limit and all the `PalmRegular` properties are conclusions.
Theorem 3 additionally makes uniqueness, intensity one and Palm regularity explicit. Its
stationarity conclusion corresponds to the translation invariance stated in the paper.

The statement uses the definitions of `Definitions.lean` in this directory, which imports
Mathlib only. The proof of the theorem is the only `sorry`.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Riesz2Audit.Convergence

noncomputable section

open MeasureTheory Filter Set
open scoped ENNReal NNReal Topology BigOperators BoundedContinuousFunction

namespace Riesz2

/-- For β > 0 and s ∈ (0,1) ∪ (1,+∞), the microscopic laws Q_{N,β} converge in total variation
on every bounded window to a unique process Riesz_{s,β}. The limit is translation invariant
and has intensity one. Its Palm law has two-sided, index-stationary gaps of mean one, as
constructed in Subsection 6.3.
This is Theorem 3 of the paper. -/
theorem convergence :
  ∀ p : Parameters, ∃ h : UniqueRieszLimit p,
    let P := limitingRieszProcess p h
    IsStationary P ∧ HasIntensity P 1 ∧ PalmRegular P :=
  sorry

end Riesz2

end

end Riesz2Audit.Convergence

end
